__pycache__/
*.pyc
.pytest_cache/
github_repositories_SYNTHETIC.csv
yeast_feats_*.csv
dgap_high_powered_results.csv
govphys_quadratic_results.csv
yeast_name_orf_map.csv

# ...but the deposited copy of the GovPhys cohort IS committed, on purpose. The
# root-level file above is a scratch output of a live GitHub fetch and stays
# ignored; cohort-audit/data/ holds the artifact produced by the pre-registered
# CI run, which is what makes the N=992 result reproducible from a clean clone.
# Ignoring it was the reason the audit had to report the gap as open.
!cohort-audit/data/govphys_quadratic_results.csv

# NOTE: yeast_interactome_DEG.csv, scer_essential_genes_DEG.csv and
# scer_essential_orfs.txt were previously ignored here. That is exactly why the
# cohort audit reported "no essentiality labels are committed anywhere" — the files
# existed on the author's disk but never entered the repository, so the outcome
# coupling was not offline-reproducible. They are now COMMITTED under data/yeast/
# (404 KB total). Do not re-ignore them: they are the evidence, not build output.
!data/yeast/yeast_interactome_DEG.csv
!data/yeast/scer_essential_genes_DEG.csv
!data/yeast/scer_essential_orfs.txt

# build artifact: generated by scripts/gen_deep_live.mjs at Vercel build time
public/deep_live_results.json
scratchpad/

# Secrets — never commit
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key

# keel: the single-file executable contains a whole copy of node (~124 MB), so
# it is BUILT, not committed. python3 keel/build_exe.py --binary makes it, and
# keel/test_exe.py proves the 34 KB bundle inside it is the tested one.
keel/dist/keel.blob
keel/dist/keel-*
keel/dist/*.exe
